// ============================================================
// SYNTAX EXPLORATION: What would truly great DX look like?
//
// Let me study the patterns from the best TS libraries:
//
// Drizzle:  pgTable('users', { id: integer().primaryKey(), name: text().notNull() })
// Zod:      z.object({ name: z.string(), age: z.number() })
// tRPC:     t.router({ getUser: publicProcedure.input(z.object({...})).query(handler) })
// Elysia:   new Elysia().get('/user/:id', handler).post('/user', handler)
// Better:   betterAuth({ database, plugins: [twoFactor] })
//
// Key patterns:
// 1. Flat structure, not nested
// 2. Chaining for constraints
// 3. Callback for composition
// 4. Type inference flows naturally
// 5. Named, not positional
//
// The current syntax's problems:
// 1. program() takes a massive nested object
// 2. Account constraints are arrays [pda, writable] — not discoverable
// 3. Logic functions have two destructured params — verbose
// 4. Token account references are strings — not type-safe
// 5. The whole thing reads like JSON, not code
// ============================================================

// ──────────────────────────────────────────────
// APPROACH 1: Drizzle-style (chaining + flat)
// ──────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// Accounts are like Drizzle tables. Instructions are like tRPC procedures.

/*
